{
  "dom_id": "step-q228738",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "The inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {4}^{3x}-5$ is\n\n${f}^{-1}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {4}^{3x}-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {4}^{3y}-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {4}^{3y}-5 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {4}^{3y} & =x+5 \\\\ {4}^{3y} & =2x+10 \\\\ {log}_{4}⁡({4}^{3y}) & ={log}_{4}⁡(2x+10) \\\\ 3y & ={log}_{4}⁡(2x+10) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{3}{log}_{4}⁡(2x+10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{3}{log}_{4}⁡(2x+10).$",
